<Positioned left="732" top="1249"><Text fontSize="14" color="#B9C7DE" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">All 46 of the 46 measured peaks fall in September or October, so the season itself never moved. The mean</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="732" top="1269"><Text fontSize="14" color="#B9C7DE" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">peak date is day 58 from 1 August for 1979–1994 and day 51 for 2000–2025 — a shift far smaller than the</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="732" top="1289"><Text fontSize="14" color="#B9C7DE" fontFamily="Noto Sans CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">year-to-year scatter.</Text></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="1346"><Container width="1604" height="1" color="#7C8CB040"/></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="1358" width="1604"><Container alignment="CENTER_LEFT"><Text fontSize="11" color="#6C7C99FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">SOURCES  S1 NASA Ozone Watch — Annual Records (Antarctic) · S2 NASA Ozone Watch — What is the Ozone Hole?</Text></Container></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="1376" width="1604"><Container alignment="CENTER_LEFT"><Text fontSize="11" color="#6C7C99FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">Marker position = the published date of that year's maximum daily hole area; marker size and the right-hand bar = the published area. The amber band is the 07 Sep – 13 Oct averaging window named in the source table. 1995 is drawn as a labelled gap because the source has no data for it.</Text></Container></Positioned>
<Positioned left="48" top="1398"><Text fontSize="11" color="#6C7C99FF" fontFamily="Noto Sans Mono CJK SC" fontStyle="NORMAL">STRATOSPHERE REVIEW · ISSUE 07 · FIG. 04</Text></Positioned>
